                'and it makes no finding about any one company\'s intent.' \
            ] \
        ] \
    ])
endfunction
unittestRunTest('testMain_companyPerson')


# All types, sorted by insider sales
async function testMain_indexInsiderSort():
    data = testData(['data/meta.json', 'data/index.json'])
    systemGlobalSet('vType', 'all')
    systemGlobalSet('vSort', 'insider')
    unittestMockAll(data)
    tiMain()
    log = unittestMockEnd()
    systemGlobalSet('vType', null)
    systemGlobalSet('vSort', null)
    unittestDeepEqual(log, [ \
        [ \
            'systemFetch', \
            [ \
                ['data/meta.json', 'data/index.json'] \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                'Index | [Group totals](#var.vPage=\'totals\'&_top) | [Threshold explorer](#var.vPage=\'explore\'&_top) | [Methodology](#var.vPage=\'method\'&_top) | [Data and limits](#var.vPage=\'data\'&_top) | [Downloads](#var.vPage=\'downloads\'&_top)' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'documentSetTitle', \
            ['The Treadmill Index'] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '# The Treadmill Index', \
                '', \
                'Large, growing companies whose revenue growth has not become meaningful cash for outside owners, because the cash', \
                'goes out as stock pay. Owner cash is free cash flow less stock-based compensation; "meaningful" is 3.0% of revenue.', \
                'Of 10 companies in the SEC data, 5 pass the screen and 4 are ranked. [How it is computed](#var.vPage=\'method\'&_top).', \
                '', \
                '## What a high rank can mean for a shareholder', \
                '', \
                '**The headline.** A company with no owner cash cannot be valued on what it earns for its owners. It is valued on revenue', \
                'growth, and on profit measures that leave stock pay out. Where that revenue is held up by spending that has to go on,', \
                'such as a large sales force, a valuation on owner cash would be far lower. At the higher valuation its insiders are', \
                'granted shares, and can sell them, every year. The view of this index is that at some companies this is the point: owner', \
                'cash is kept near zero so that the company is never valued on it, while insiders turn grants into cash at the price that', \
                'results. Shareholders who take "investing in growth" on trust bear the cost. The numbers cannot say at which companies', \
                'this is deliberate: a real bet on growth looks the same until it pays off.', \
                '', \
                '**The three signs.** The index looks for three things together, each one visible in the filings. A real growth bet', \
                'loses them in time: owner cash appears as growth slows, costs fall against revenue, and insiders keep what they are granted.', \
                '', \
                '1. **Owner cash that stays near zero (P1).** Together the 5 companies here recorded $6.5B of stock pay against $5.2B of free cash flow;', \
                '   leaving out the capital-led companies, $5.3B against $4.1B. At 5 of the 5, owner cash has been under 3.0% of revenue for five or more fiscal years running.', \
                '2. **Revenue held up by costs.** Hiring in sales, marketing and development can absorb whatever growth brings in. At 0 of the 4 ranked companies,', \
                '   operating costs took all of gross profit or more in the latest year; 3 kept them flat or rising as a share of revenue over five years. Cash that does arrive can go to buying back the', \
                '   shares just granted: of the 1 that bought back shares over five years and whose share count can', \
                '   be followed, the count still rose at 1, after $40.0M spent on buybacks and employees\' vesting taxes.', \
                [ \
                    '3. **Insiders who are granted shares and sell them (P3).** In 2021‑2025, the officers and directors of 2 of these companies sold $114K of shares on the open market,', \
                    '   and were granted shares worth $110K and options over shares worth $300K at the time. Over the same years those companies produced -$483.0M of owner cash.', \
                    '   At 2 of the 2, insiders sold more than the business produced for its owners; among the cash-generative companies it was 1 of 1.' \
                ], \
                '', \
                '**What it costs a shareholder.** Each share owns a smaller part of a company that produces no cash for its owners, at a', \
                'price that cannot be checked against what it earns. A falling share price makes it worse: the same pay takes more shares.', \
                '', \
                '**What a rank does not prove.** A rank measures a pattern. It is not a finding about any one company\'s purpose, and not', \
                'a forecast that its shares will fall. 0 of the 5 had meaningful owner cash in their latest 12 months, and 1 are capital-led: their', \
                'shortfall is mostly investment. The figures in the second and third signs leave the capital-led companies out. Each', \
                'company page shows which way its own numbers point, and what cuts the other way. [Group totals](#var.vPage=\'totals\'&_top) has every figure, with the', \
                'cash-generative companies beside them as a baseline.', \
                '', \
                '## The index' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '**Show:** [Stock pay \\(5\\)](#var.vSort=\'insider\') | [Thin margin \\(1\\)](#var.vSort=\'insider\'&var.vType=\'thin_margin\') | [Cash burner \\(1\\)](#var.vSort=\'insider\'&var.vType=\'cash_burner\') | [Cash generative \\(1\\)](#var.vSort=\'insider\'&var.vType=\'cash_generative\') | [Excluded industry \\(1\\)](#var.vSort=\'insider\'&var.vType=\'excluded\') | All (9) \\\n**Sort:** [Three signs](#var.vType=\'all\') | [Screen rank](#var.vSort=\'rank\'&var.vType=\'all\') | [P1](#var.vSort=\'p1\'&var.vType=\'all\') | [Cumulative margin](#var.vSort=\'cum\'&var.vType=\'all\') | [Latest margin](#var.vSort=\'latest\'&var.vType=\'all\') | [Stock pay](#var.vSort=\'pay\'&var.vType=\'all\') | [Revenue](#var.vSort=\'revenue\'&var.vType=\'all\') | Insider sales | [Sales / grants](#var.vSort=\'ratio\'&var.vType=\'all\') | [Name](#var.vSort=\'name\'&var.vType=\'all\')', \
                '', \
                '*All companies that pass the basic tests.*' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'elementModelRender', \
            [ \
                { \
                    'elem': [ \
                        {'elem': {'text': 'Find a company or ticker: '}, 'html': 'b'}, \
                        { \
                            'attr': { \
                                'autocomplete': 'off', \
                                'id': 'tiFind', \
                                'size': 30, \
                                'style': 'font-size: inherit; border: thin solid black; padding: 0.4em;', \
                                'type': 'text', \
                                'value': '' \
                            }, \
                            'callback': {'keyup': '<function>'}, \
                            'html': 'input' \
                        }, \
                        {'text': ' '}, \
                        { \
                            'attr': {'style': 'cursor: pointer; user-select: none;'}, \
                            'callback': {'click': '<function>'}, \
                            'elem': {'text': 'Find'}, \
                            'html': 'a' \
                        }, \
                        [] \
                    ], \
                    'html': 'p' \
                } \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                [ \
                    '| Rank | Company | Ticker | Three signs (75) | Owner cash, P1 (25) | Costs (25) | Insiders, P3 (25) | Screen score (50) and rank | Cumulative owner-cash margin | Latest owner-cash margin | Stock pay / revenue | Insider sales | Insider sales / grants | Years | Notes | Type |', \
                    '| --: | :-- | :-- | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: | :-- | :-- | :-- |', \
                    '|  | [Cash Co](#var.vCik=4&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) | CASH | 0.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 | 13.0 | 0.0 | 8.0% | 8.0% | 2.0% | $1.5B | n/a | 2019‑2025 |  | Cash generative |', \
                    '|  | [Bank Co](#var.vCik=5&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) | BANK | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | n/a | -10.0% | -10.0% | 20.0% | $1.2M | n/a | 2019‑2025 |  | Excluded industry |', \
                    '| 1 | [Stock Pay Co](#var.vCik=1&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) | SPAY | 36.5 | 18.6 | 21.5 | 2.4 | 37.3 (2) | -2.0% | -2.0% | 12.0% | $110K | 0.3x | 2019‑2025 |  | Stock pay |', \
                    '|  | [One\\-Off Co](#var.vCik=6&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) | OOC | n/a | 21.1 | 21.5 | n/a | 42.2 (1) | -4.0% | -4.0% | 13.0% | $4K | n/a | 2019‑2025 | O F S | Stock pay |', \
                    '|  | [Capital & Sons \\[Holdings\\]](#var.vCik=2&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) |  | n/a | 18.6 | 16.7 | n/a | 32.6 (3) | -2.0% | -2.0% | 12.0% | n/a | n/a | 2019‑2025 | C I | Stock pay |', \
                    '|  | [Marginal Co](#var.vCik=10&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) | MARG | n/a | 13.0 | 21.5 | n/a | 25.9 (4) | 2.6% | 2.6% | 7.4% | n/a | n/a | 2019‑2025 | I | Stock pay |', \
                    '|  | [Burner Co](#var.vCik=8&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) | BURN | n/a | 25.0 | 21.5 | n/a | 50.0 | -13.0% | -13.0% | 12.0% | n/a | n/a | 2019‑2025 | I | Cash burner |', \
                    '|  | [No Cost Line Co](#var.vCik=3&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) | NCL | n/a | 24.0 | n/a | n/a | n/a | -8.2% | -8.0% | 20.0% | n/a | n/a | 2019‑2025 | F N X I | Stock pay |', \
                    '|  | [Thin Co](#var.vCik=7&var.vPage=\'company\'&_top) | THIN | n/a | 15.4 | 21.5 | n/a | 30.9 | 0.6% | 0.6% | 1.9% | n/a | n/a | 2019‑2025 | I | Thin margin |' \
                ] \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '**Rank** is by the three-signs score, among the companies that pass the screen and have all three pillars. Unranked', \
                'companies are listed for comparison only. **Cumulative** covers every fiscal year shown; **latest** is the latest 12 months.', \
                '', \
                '**The three-signs score is out of 75.** It is P1 + (P1 / 25) x (costs + P3): costs and insider selling count only to', \
                'the extent owners are getting no cash. The costs sign is half whether operating costs fell against revenue (P2b) and', \
                'half how much of gross profit they take. The score is provisional: the costs level is new, and insider grants are valued', \
                'at the full price of the shares they cover, which understates how much of their grants insiders sold.', \
                '**Insider sales** are open-market sales by officers and directors over five calendar years, and **sales / grants**', \
                'compares them with the shares and options granted over the same years; each company page has the detail.', \
                '', \
                '**The screen score is out of 50**, and is the agreed definition\'s whole-market score: P1 + (P1 / 25) x (P2b scaled to 25).', \
                'Its rank is in brackets. The full score of the definition, out of 100, also needs headcount and earnings-release framing', \
                'for the top 25; those need hand-checked data and are not measured. The three scores must not be compared with each other.', \
                '', \
                '**Neighbouring ranks are close.** Moving one threshold of the definition by a fifth changes the top ten in 0 of 32 tests.', \
                'Read a rank as a rough position, not a precise one. [Data and limits](#var.vPage=\'data\'&_top) lists every change.', \
                '', \
                '**Notes**', \
                '' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '- **C** - Capital-led: capital spending is more than twice stock pay, so the shortfall is mostly investment.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '- **O** - Inventory, lending or a discontinued business moved operating cash flow by more than a quarter of revenue in at least one year. The company page shows P1 with and without those years.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '- **F** - The latest fiscal year stands in for the latest 12 months, because the quarterly data is unusable or there is no later quarter.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '- **N** - The cost line cannot be built from the filing tags, so there is no P2b and no screen score. The company has P1 alone and is unranked.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '- **X** - No capital spending tag in at least one year; that year counts capital spending as zero.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            ['- **G** - At least one fiscal year is missing between the first and last year.'] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '- **J** - Revenue more than tripled in one year, which often marks a merger or a change of reporting entity.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '- **S** - The share count jumps in one year (a stock split, or a conversion at listing). Share counts are not adjusted.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '- **I** - No insider transactions in the SEC data sets for the five years. Foreign companies have not had to file them.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '---', \
                '', \
                'Data as of 2026-11-05, from SEC filings. Method version 1.0.0.', \
                'History starts about 2009, when the SEC began collecting financial statements as data.', \
                '', \
                'The index ranks how outside owners have been treated. It is not a short list, a price forecast or investment advice,', \
                'and it makes no finding about any one company\'s intent.' \
            ] \
        ] \
    ])
endfunction
unittestRunTest('testMain_indexInsiderSort')


