# The index page of a build without the insider data sets
async function testMain_indexNoInsider():
    data = testData(['data/meta.json', 'data/index.json'], 'noinsider/')
    unittestMockAll(data)
    tiMain()
    log = unittestMockEnd()
    unittestDeepEqual(log, [ \
        [ \
            'systemFetch', \
            [ \
                ['data/meta.json', 'data/index.json'] \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                'Index | [Group totals](#var.vPage=\'totals\'&_top) | [Threshold explorer](#var.vPage=\'explore\'&_top) | [Methodology](#var.vPage=\'method\'&_top) | [Data and limits](#var.vPage=\'data\'&_top) | [Downloads](#var.vPage=\'downloads\'&_top)' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'documentSetTitle', \
            ['The Treadmill Index'] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '# The Treadmill Index', \
                '', \
                'Large, growing companies whose revenue growth has not become meaningful cash for outside owners, because the cash', \
                'goes out as stock pay. Owner cash is free cash flow less stock-based compensation; "meaningful" is 3.0% of revenue.', \
                'Of 10 companies in the SEC data, 5 pass the screen and 4 are ranked. [How it is computed](#var.vPage=\'method\'&_top).', \
                '', \
                '## What a high rank can mean for a shareholder', \
                '', \
                '**The headline.** A company with no owner cash cannot be valued on what it earns for its owners. It is valued on revenue', \
                'growth, and on profit measures that leave stock pay out. Where that revenue is held up by spending that has to go on,', \
                'such as a large sales force, a valuation on owner cash would be far lower. At the higher valuation its insiders are', \
                'granted shares, and can sell them, every year. The view of this index is that at some companies this is the point: owner', \
                'cash is kept near zero so that the company is never valued on it, while insiders turn grants into cash at the price that', \
                'results. Shareholders who take "investing in growth" on trust bear the cost. The numbers cannot say at which companies', \
                'this is deliberate: a real bet on growth looks the same until it pays off.', \
                '', \
                '**The three signs.** The index looks for three things together, each one visible in the filings. A real growth bet', \
                'loses them in time: owner cash appears as growth slows, costs fall against revenue, and insiders keep what they are granted.', \
                '', \
                '1. **Owner cash that stays near zero (P1).** Together the 5 companies here recorded $6.5B of stock pay against $5.2B of free cash flow;', \
                '   leaving out the capital-led companies, $5.3B against $4.1B. At 5 of the 5, owner cash has been under 3.0% of revenue for five or more fiscal years running.', \
                '2. **Revenue held up by costs.** Hiring in sales, marketing and development can absorb whatever growth brings in. At 0 of the 4 ranked companies,', \
                '   operating costs took all of gross profit or more in the latest year; 3 kept them flat or rising as a share of revenue over five years. Cash that does arrive can go to buying back the', \
                '   shares just granted: of the 1 that bought back shares over five years and whose share count can', \
                '   be followed, the count still rose at 1, after $40.0M spent on buybacks and employees\' vesting taxes.', \
                '3. **Insiders who are granted shares and sell them (P3).** Insider grants and sales are not measured in this build.', \
                '', \
                '**What it costs a shareholder.** Each share owns a smaller part of a company that produces no cash for its owners, at a', \
                'price that cannot be checked against what it earns. A falling share price makes it worse: the same pay takes more shares.', \
                '', \
                '**What a rank does not prove.** A rank measures a pattern. It is not a finding about any one company\'s purpose, and not', \
                'a forecast that its shares will fall. 0 of the 5 had meaningful owner cash in their latest 12 months, and 1 are capital-led: their', \
