# The methodology page
async function testMain_method():
    data = testData(['data/meta.json'])
    systemGlobalSet('vPage', 'method')
    unittestMockAll(data)
    tiMain()
    log = unittestMockEnd()
    systemGlobalSet('vPage', null)
    unittestDeepEqual(log, [ \
        [ \
            'systemFetch', \
            [ \
                ['data/meta.json'] \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '[Index](#var=&_top) | [Group totals](#var.vPage=\'totals\'&_top) | [Threshold explorer](#var.vPage=\'explore\'&_top) | Methodology | [Data and limits](#var.vPage=\'data\'&_top) | [Downloads](#var.vPage=\'downloads\'&_top)' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'documentSetTitle', \
            ['Methodology - The Treadmill Index'] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '# Methodology', \
                '', \
                '## What the index measures', \
                '', \
                'Mature companies whose revenue growth has not become meaningful cash for outside owners because the cash goes out as', \
                'stock pay. It ranks how owners have been treated. It does not predict share prices, it is not a trading', \
                'recommendation, and it does not measure intent: the view that some companies keep the pattern going on purpose is', \
                'this index\'s, stated on the index page, and is not a finding about any one company. Paying staff in stock, and', \
                'reporting profit before stock pay, are legal and disclosed.', \
                '', \
                '## Definitions', \
                '', \
                '- **Free cash flow** = operating cash flow - capital spending (including capitalized software).', \
                '- **Stock pay** = stock-based compensation expense.', \
                '- **Owner cash** = free cash flow - stock pay.', \
                '- **Owner-cash margin** = owner cash / revenue.', \
                '- **Meaningful** owner cash is 3.0% of revenue or more. It is not zero, so a company cannot pass by a hair.', \
                '', \
                'Annual figures come from the financial data companies file with the SEC. Where a figure was filed more than once,', \
                'the most recently filed value is used. Nothing is estimated; a figure that is not in the filings is shown as "n/a".', \
                '', \
                '## Stage 1: the market screen', \
                '', \
                'Every company in the SEC data is tested. A company is kept if all of these hold:', \
                '', \
                [ \
                    '| Test | Companies left |', \
                    '| :-- | --: |', \
                    '| In the SEC financial data file | 10 |', \
                    '| Ever reported $0.5B of annual revenue, and reports stock pay | 10 |', \
                    '| Still filing: the latest fiscal year ended within 548 days of the data date | 10 |', \
                    '| Latest annual revenue of $1.0B or more | 10 |', \
                    '| 5 or more years of data | 10 |', \
                    '| Revenue up 50.0% or more over those years | 9 |', \
                    '| Not in an excluded industry | 8 |', \
                    '| Cumulative owner-cash margin under 3.0% | 7 |', \
                    '| Stock pay is the cause | 5 |', \
                    '| ...and both pillars of the screen score can be built (ranked) | 4 |' \
                ], \
                '', \
                '**Excluded industries**, by SIC code: drug developer (2834); drug developer (2836); bank (6000-6099); lender (6100-6199); broker or asset manager (6200-6289); insurer (6300-6399); asset manager (6722-6726); REIT (6798); asset manager (6799). Their cash flows include customer money,', \
                'loans or property, so free cash flow does not mean the same thing.', \
                '', \
                '**Stock pay is the cause** when cumulative free cash flow is zero or positive, stock pay is at least 2.0% of revenue, and stock pay is at least 50.0% of free cash flow. Without this rule the screen is', \
                'dominated by distributors, retailers and companies that burn cash before any stock pay. Those are listed', \
                'separately, unranked, as "thin margin" and "cash burner".', \
                '', \
                '**Capital-led** companies are flagged, not removed: their capital spending is more than 2 times their stock pay, so their shortfall is mostly investment.', \
                '', \
                '## The screen score (50 points)', \
                '', \
                'Points are linear between the two values given, and capped.', \
                '', \
                '**P1. No meaningful cash for owners (25 points)**', \
                '', \
                '- Share of years with an owner-cash margin under 3.0%: 0 to 10 points.', \
                '- Cumulative owner-cash margin: 0 points at 5.0%, 8 at -10.0%.', \
                '- Latest-12-month owner-cash margin: 0 points at 5.0%, 7 at -5.0%.', \
                '  The latest 12 months are the latest fiscal year, plus the year-to-date period reported since, less the same', \
                '  period a year earlier. If the quarterly figures are unusable, the latest fiscal year is used and the row is noted.', \
                '', \
                'Where inventory, lending or a discontinued business moved operating cash flow by more than 25.0% of revenue in a year, the company page shows P1 with and', \
                'without those years. The ranking uses all years.', \
                '', \
                '**P2b. Revenue that needs matching costs (13 points, scaled to 25)**', \
                '', \
                '- Operating expenses excluding cost of revenue, as a percent of revenue, compared between the first and last of the', \
                '  latest 5 fiscal years: 13 points if flat or up, 0 if down 10 points.', \
                '- The cost line is built the same way in both years, by the first of these that works: gross profit - operating income; total costs - cost of revenue; revenue - operating income - cost of revenue.', \
                '- If it cannot be built, the company has P1 alone and is unranked. Missing is not treated as zero.', \
                '', \
                '**Screen score = P1 + (P1 / 25) x (P2b scaled to 25)**', \
                '', \
                'P2b counts only to the extent owners are getting no cash. Flat costs are common at cash-rich companies too, so on', \
                'their own they say nothing.', \
                '', \
                '**Three-signs score (75 points) = P1 + (P1 / 25) x (costs + P3)**', \
                '', \
                'The index page\'s default order, because the three signs together are what the index is looking for: no cash for', \
                'owners (P1), revenue held up by costs, and insiders who are granted shares and sell them (P3, described below). Of the 4 ranked companies, 1 have all three.', \
                '', \
                'The **costs sign (25 points)** has two halves. One is P2b, scaled to 12.5: whether operating costs fell against revenue.', \
                'The other is their level: operating costs excluding cost of revenue as a share of gross profit in the latest fiscal', \
                'year, 0 points at 70.0% and 12.5 at 100.0% or more. P2b alone gives nothing to a company whose', \
                'spending is very high but falling; the level does. Among the companies that produce meaningful owner cash, operating', \
                'costs typically take about two thirds of gross profit.', \
                '', \
                'The three-signs score is provisional. The costs level and P3 are new measures whose scales are untested judgment, and', \
                'they are not part of the agreed definition\'s screen score. Each company page also reports sales and marketing,', \
                'research and development, and general and administrative spending as a share of revenue where the company tags them;', \
                'these are not scored.', \
                '', \
                '## Why these choices', \
                '', \
                '- The cost test uses the latest five years because a full-history test rewards any company that listed young.', \
                '- Points scale with size because hard cut-offs made ranks flip on small changes. The [data and limits](#var.vPage=\'data\'&_top) page shows which top-ten ranks still move', \
                '  when a threshold moves by a fifth, and the [threshold explorer](#var.vPage=\'explore\'&_top) lets you move them.', \
                '', \
                '## Not in this release', \
                '', \
                'The full index (100 points) for the top 25 adds three pillars. No full score is published, and their point scales are', \
                'untested judgment:', \
                '', \
                '- **P2a.** Real gross profit per employee, annual growth. Needs headcount from annual reports. Not measured.', \
                '- **P3.** Insiders granting and selling, over the latest 5 calendar years: the share of', \
                '  years with both new grants and open-market sales (0 to 12 points), and', \
                '  open-market sales as a multiple of grants (0 points at 0.5 times, 13 at 1.5 times).', \
                '  **Measured, and shown on each company page as provisional.** The definition calls for the grant-date fair value of', \
                '  grants, which the SEC data does not give consistently. Grants are valued instead at the market price of the shares', \
                '  they cover, which overstates options and so understates the multiple.', \
                '- **P4.** Headline numbers that leave out stock pay: stock pay as a share of adjusted profit in earnings releases.', \
                '  Not measured.', \
                '', \
                'Also not done: confirming each company was founded ten or more years ago, combining predecessor companies,', \
                'adjusting share counts for splits, valuation, and governance facts.', \
                '', \
                '## Glossary', \
                '', \
                [ \
                    '- **Owner cash.** Free cash flow less stock pay: the cash a year of business leaves for the people who own the shares.', \
                    '- **Free cash flow.** Cash from operations less capital spending. It does not subtract stock pay, because stock pay uses no cash.', \
                    '- **Stock pay.** Stock-based compensation: wages paid in shares and options. It costs owners a share of the company, not the company\'s cash.', \
                    '- **Meaningful owner cash.** Owner cash of at least the stated share of revenue.', \
                    '- **Capital-led.** A company whose capital spending is more than twice its stock pay. Its shortfall is mostly investment.', \
                    '- **Insider.** An officer or director who must report trades in the company to the SEC.', \
                    '- **Grant.** Shares, share units or options awarded to an insider as pay.', \
                    '- **Open-market sale.** A sale on the exchange. It leaves out shares withheld for taxes, gifts, and shares sold back to the company.', \
                    '- **Buyback.** A company buying its own shares. It returns cash to owners only if it leaves fewer shares.', \
                    '- **Treadmill.** The pattern the index looks for: no cash for owners, revenue held up by costs, and insiders who are granted shares and sell them, year after year.', \
                    '- **Latest 12 months.** The latest fiscal year, plus the part-year reported since, less the same part of the year before.' \
                ], \
                '', \
                '## Rules', \
                '', \
                '- Every published figure traces to an SEC filing, linked from the company page.', \
                '- The index includes what cuts the other way: recent improvement, one-off causes and cash returned.', \
                '- No statement here says a company\'s reporting is false or misleading. Only a court or regulator can find that.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '---', \
                '', \
                'Data as of 2026-11-05, from SEC filings. Method version 1.0.0.', \
                'History starts about 2009, when the SEC began collecting financial statements as data.', \
                '', \
                'The index ranks how outside owners have been treated. It is not a short list, a price forecast or investment advice,', \
                'and it makes no finding about any one company\'s intent.' \
            ] \
        ] \
    ])
endfunction
unittestRunTest('testMain_method')


# The data and limits page
async function testMain_data():
    data = testData(['data/meta.json'])
    systemGlobalSet('vPage', 'data')
    unittestMockAll(data)
    tiMain()
    log = unittestMockEnd()
    systemGlobalSet('vPage', null)
    unittestDeepEqual(log, [ \
        [ \
            'systemFetch', \
            [ \
                ['data/meta.json'] \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '[Index](#var=&_top) | [Group totals](#var.vPage=\'totals\'&_top) | [Threshold explorer](#var.vPage=\'explore\'&_top) | [Methodology](#var.vPage=\'method\'&_top) | Data and limits | [Downloads](#var.vPage=\'downloads\'&_top)' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'documentSetTitle', \
            ['Data and limits - The Treadmill Index'] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '# Data and limits', \
                '', \
                '## Sources', \
                '', \
                '- Financial figures: the SEC bulk file [companyfacts.zip](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/daily-index/xbrl/companyfacts.zip). The latest filing in', \
                '  it is dated **2026-11-05**; that is the as-of date of everything here.', \
                '  File size 123,456,789 bytes, SHA-256 `84809832c335d64f039cb04e746014ac3b0fa01abe787f3b4e061f65d58c9263`.', \
                '- Industry codes, tickers and former names: the [SEC company API](https://data.sec.gov/submissions/).', \
                '- Insider transactions: the SEC [insider transactions data sets](https://www.sec.gov/data-research/sec-markets-data/insider-transactions-data-sets), 1 quarterly files from `2025q4_form345.zip` to `2025q4_form345.zip`.', \
                '- Method version 1.0.0. A figure cited from this page can be reproduced from the same', \
                '  SEC file and the same method version.', \
                '', \
                '## What is in the data', \
                '', \
                [ \
                    '| Type | Companies | Meaning |', \
                    '| :-- | --: | :-- |', \
                    '| Stock pay | 5 | Passed the screen: owner cash is under the line and stock pay is the cause. |', \
                    '| Thin margin | 1 | Owner cash is under the line, but stock pay is too small to be the cause. |', \
                    '| Cash burner | 1 | Free cash flow is negative before any stock pay. |', \
                    '| Cash generative | 1 | Owner cash is at or above the line. These companies are the baseline. |', \
                    '| Excluded industry | 1 | A bank, insurer, lender, broker, asset manager, REIT or drug developer. |' \
                ], \
                '', \
                'Excluded industries: bank 1.', \
                '', \
                '## Known limits', \
                '', \
                '- **History starts about 2009.** The SEC has collected financial statements as data only since then. A company\'s', \
                '  "whole period" is the years it has in that data, which can be shorter than its life.', \
                '- **Fiscal years.** A fiscal year ending in January, February or March is labelled with the prior calendar year.', \
                '- **Revenue** is the largest of four revenue tags for the year. Pass-through revenue (for example, the face value', \
                '  of cryptocurrency sold) makes revenue-based ratios look smaller.', \
                '- **Capital spending** is purchases of property and equipment (or of productive assets) plus capitalized software.', \
                '  A year with neither tag counts as zero and is noted.', \
                '- **The cost line** cannot be built for 1 companies (1 of those that pass the screen) because of inconsistent tagging. They are unranked.', \
                '- **Latest 12 months** fall back to the latest fiscal year when a quarterly figure is missing or tagged differently.', \
                '- **Industry codes** are the SEC\'s and are coarse. Securities brokers are excluded along with asset managers, because', \
                '  they hold customer money. Insurance agents and brokers are not excluded: they are not insurers. Some real-estate and', \
                '  payment companies are not excluded.', \
                '- **Restated years.** Each year uses its most recently filed value. When a company restates recent years for a', \
                '  business it has left, older years are not restated, so the two can sit side by side.', \
                '- **Foreign companies** that file financial data with the SEC in US dollars are included.', \
                '- **Predecessor companies** are not combined, and **share counts** are not adjusted for splits.', \
                '- **Founding dates** are not in the data. Some listed companies are less than ten years old.', \
                '- **Capital-led companies** pass the screen but their shortfall is mostly capital spending. They are flagged.', \
                '- **Insiders** are officers and directors who file as people. A filing made with an entity counts only if one of', \
                '  its people is an officer, and an outside director\'s shares held through an entity are left out unless the filing', \
                '  says the holder is a trust, family or a retirement account. Some fund sales may still be counted, and some personal', \
                '  holdings left out.', \
                '- **Insider grants** are valued at the market price of the shares they cover, not at their accounting fair value.', \
                '  Options and performance awards are overstated. Amended insider filings are not used. A price far from the', \
                '  company\'s other reported prices is treated as a filing error and left out.', \
                '', \
                '## If a threshold moves by a fifth', \
                '', \
                'Each threshold was moved down and up by a fifth, one at a time, and the index rebuilt. These are the changes in the', \
                'top ten by three-signs score.', \
                '', \
                ['| Threshold moved | Value | Changes in the top ten |', '| :-- | :-- | :-- |'], \
                '', \
                'No change in the top ten: Least latest revenue, a fifth lower; Least latest revenue, a fifth higher; Least years of data, a fifth lower; Least years of data, a fifth higher; Least revenue growth, a fifth lower; Least revenue growth, a fifth higher; Meaningful owner cash, a fifth lower; Meaningful owner cash, a fifth higher; Least stock pay, share of revenue, a fifth lower; Least stock pay, share of revenue, a fifth higher; Least stock pay, share of free cash flow, a fifth lower; Least stock pay, share of free cash flow, a fifth higher; P1 cumulative margin that scores 0, a fifth lower; P1 cumulative margin that scores 0, a fifth higher; P1 cumulative margin that scores 8, a fifth lower; P1 cumulative margin that scores 8, a fifth higher; P1 latest margin that scores 0, a fifth lower; P1 latest margin that scores 0, a fifth higher; P1 latest margin that scores 7, a fifth lower; P1 latest margin that scores 7, a fifth higher; P2b window, in years, a fifth lower; P2b window, in years, a fifth higher; P2b cost fall that scores 0, a fifth lower; P2b cost fall that scores 0, a fifth higher; Costs as a share of gross profit that score 0, a fifth lower; Costs as a share of gross profit that score 0, a fifth higher; Costs as a share of gross profit that score in full, a fifth lower; Costs as a share of gross profit that score in full, a fifth higher; P3 sales as a multiple of grants that score 0, a fifth lower; P3 sales as a multiple of grants that score 0, a fifth higher; P3 sales as a multiple of grants that score 13, a fifth lower; P3 sales as a multiple of grants that score 13, a fifth higher.', \
                '', \
                'The thresholds, at the definition\'s values: `capital_led_ratio` = 2, `cost_level_full` = 1, `cost_level_zero` = 0.7, `growth_min` = 0.5, `meaningful_margin` = 0.03, `one_off_ocf_share` = 0.25, `p1_cum_full` = -0.1, `p1_cum_points` = 8, `p1_cum_zero` = 0.05, `p1_latest_full` = -0.05, `p1_latest_points` = 7, `p1_latest_zero` = 0.05, `p1_years_points` = 10, `p2b_points` = 13, `p2b_window_years` = 5, `p2b_zero_drop` = 10, `p3_price_days` = 90, `p3_ratio_full` = 1.5, `p3_ratio_points` = 13, `p3_ratio_zero` = 0.5, `p3_unvalued_max` = 0.05, `p3_years` = 5, `p3_years_points` = 12, `prefilter_revenue` = 500000000, `revenue_min` = 1000000000, `sbc_fcf_min` = 0.5, `sbc_revenue_min` = 0.02, `still_filing_days` = 548, `years_min` = 5.', \
                '', \
                '## Change log', \
                '', \
                [ \
                    '- **Method version 1.0.0.** First release. Stage 1 screen and screen score (P1 and P2b) from SEC financial data. Insider grants and sales from SEC Forms 4 and 5, with a provisional P3. A provisional three-signs score: P1, a costs sign, and P3.' \
                ], \
                '', \
                '## Corrections', \
                '', \
                'If a figure here does not match the linked SEC filing, it is an error in this index. Report it at the', \
                '[project repository](https://github.com/craigahobbs/treadmill-index/issues) with the company and the year.' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '---', \
                '', \
                'Data as of 2026-11-05, from SEC filings. Method version 1.0.0.', \
                'History starts about 2009, when the SEC began collecting financial statements as data.', \
                '', \
                'The index ranks how outside owners have been treated. It is not a short list, a price forecast or investment advice,', \
                'and it makes no finding about any one company\'s intent.' \
            ] \
        ] \
    ])
endfunction
unittestRunTest('testMain_data')


